Thus, introducing the concept of error signal makes the back propagation process more comprehensible, as only the
error signal propagates backward through the network. The changing amount of the weight is proportional to the
product of the error signal of its destination unit and the output of its source unit. To summarise, the forward pass
calculates the network output, while the backward pass updates the weights to minimise the error. The method used in
backward pass is called back propagation, and a forward pass together with a backward pass is regarded as one loop.
The training process continue until some stopping criteria is met, such as the error function is small enough, or, it
stops to decrease after a certain number of loops.
16
Temporal difference learning
16.1
Overview
In a prediction-learning problem 15, a learning agent performs a sequence of actions, obtaining some rewards or penalties (punishments). The temporal credit assignment problem is to assign credit or blame to the situations and actions
15Using past experience with an incompletely known system to predict its future behaviour.
194
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
involved. However, it is generally difﬁcult to perform when the reinforcement delay is long and the environment is
stochastic. The most important learning paradigm has been supervised learning paradigm (SLP) (see Section (??)),
where the learner needs to associate pairs of items. When presented with just the ﬁrst item, the learner recalls the second one. Any prediction problem can be cast in a SLP by taking the ﬁrst item to be the input of a prediction function,
and the second item to be the actual outcome. That is, y  fpxq for the pair px, yq. However, Sutton [1988] argued
that these methods are inadequate and that temporal difference (TD) are far preferable.
Nowadays, the most popular procedure for temporal credit assignment is temporal difference (TD). While conventional prediction-learning methods, such as supervised learning methods (see Section (15.1)), are driven by the error
between predicted and actual outcomes, TD methods are driven by the error between temporally successive predictions. Several TD algorithms have been proposed (see Samuel [1959], Hampson [1983], Barto et al. [1983], Holland
[1986]). Sutton [1988] proved their convergence and optimality for special cases and related them to supervised
learning methods.
We can distinguish between two kinds of prediction-learning problem:
1. Single-step: all information about the correctness of each prediction is revealed at once. It is a single point
forecast. For example, predict next day stock prices based on today’s prices.
2. Multi-step: correctness is not revealed until more than one step after the prediction is made. Only partial
information relevant to its correctness is revealed at each step. For example, predict X-days ahead weather
forecast.
In type r1s, the data comes in observation-outcome pairs, which is ideally suited for pairwise supervised learning approach. TD methods are more concerned with type r2s prediction problems since type r1s is a special case. Nonetheless, many single-step prediction problems are more naturally viewed as multi-step problems. This is because patterns
are not unrelated, but are part of a series of related patterns, all providing information about the same classiﬁcation.
Sutton [1988] showed that TD methods were more efﬁcient than supervised learning methods when the data sequences
were generated by a dynamical system.
TD methods were originally used to predict the probability of winning a game for each possible board situation. As
an example, we consider the learning problem of predicting the wining probability of a tic-tac-toe by collecting all the
training instances. Given the training instance in Figure (43), suppose that the estimated winning probabilities from
states A, B and C are 0.8, 0.3, and 0.6, respectively. The player started at state A moved to B and then to C, and won
the game.
Figure 43: A sequence of state transitions with estimated probabilities in a game play.
In that setting, the delta rule (see Section (15.1.4)) would increase the WP (see Equation (15.82)) from states A, B, and
C by p1  0.8qα, p1  0.3qα, and p1  0.6qα, where α is a learning rate. Here, the learning changes are proportional
to the differences between the current predictions and the actual outcome (in this case 1). Storing the WPs in a lookup
table, and sufﬁcient training data, the delta rule would eventually converge to the true WP from every state.
On the other hand, TDp0q minimises the error (TD error) between successive predictions. Let ∆s be the learning
change to the WP from a state s. TDp0q makes the following learning changes:
∆A  p0.3  0.8qα , ∆B  p0.6  0.3qα , ∆C  p1.0  0.6qα
