Dayan [?] proved convergence of TDpλq. Note, TDp1q makes the same learning changes as the supervised learning
method. As λ approaches 1, TDpλq behaves more like supervised learning. Further, TDpλq not only males prediction
changes based on information from the immediately successive state, but also information from all the future states.
Thus, TDpλq results in faster learning than TDp0q at the early learning stage.
16.2
The TD family of learning procedures
The data sequences are generated by absorbing Markov processes, where the next state depends only on the current
state. They are deﬁned by a set of terminal states T, a set of nonterminal states N, and a set of transition probabilities
pij, i P N, j P N Y T, where each pij is the probability of a transition from state i to state j, given that the process
is in state i. Since it is absorbing, all sequences eventually terminate. Given an initial state q1, the absorbing Markov
process generates the sequence q1, q2, ..., qm 1, where qm 1 P T. The learning algorithms do not know about the
state sequence, but only of a related observation-outcome sequence x1, x2, ..., xm, z.
16.2.1
The TDp1q procedure
Consider a multi-step problem with experience coming in observation-outcome sequences of the form x1, x2, ..., xm, z,
where each xt is a vector of observable input at time t in the sequence, and z P R is the outcome of that sequence.
Note, z only depends on the terminal state qm 1, that is, z is FT -measurable. A training set consists of several
sequences tx1,p, x2,p, ..., xm,p, zpu, for p P r1, Ps. For each such sequence, the learner produces a sequence of predictions P1, P2, ..., Pm, each of which being an estimate of z. For simplicity, it is assumed that Pt is a function of xt
and a vector of weights w, that is, Pt  Ppxt, wq.
Remark 16.1 While Pt  Ppxt, wq is a function of xt and w, possibly a perceptron or a two-layers network, it is not
a multi-layer network.
All learning procedures can be expressed as rules for updating w. We want to ﬁnd rules for updating the vector w.
It is assumed that w does not change during a sequence. For each observation, an increment to w, denoted ∆wt is
computed. After a complete sequence has been run, w is modiﬁed as follows:
