Sutton [1988] proved convergence of the linear TDp0q procedure. He devised an asymptotic convergence theorem
for TDp0q when presented with new data sequences (generated by absorbing Markov processes), and a theorem that
TDp0q converges under repeated presentations of optimal estimates.
16.2.3
A random-walk example
As discussed in Appendix (16.1), Sutton [1988] showed that TD methods were more efﬁcient than supervised learning
methods when the data sequences were generated by a dynamical system. As an example, the author considered a
bounded random walk, that is, a random walk taking steps to the right or to the left with equal probability, until a
boundary is reached. This is illustrated in Figure (44), where the walk start in state D and ends if either edge state A
or G is reached. A typical walk (path) might be DCDEFG.
Figure 44: A bounded random walk
We want to ﬁnd the conditional probability of a walk reaching state G, given that it is in each of the other states.
The outcome is z  0 for a walk ending in state A and z  1 if it ends in state G. For each nonterminal state i, we
associate an observation vector Xi. If the walk is in state i at time t, then xt  Xi. For example, XD  p0, 0, 1, 0, 0qJ.
The sequence of the above typical walk becomes XD, XC, XD, XE, XF , 1. Thus, at time t and state i, for a linear
prediction Pt  w  xt, we simply get the value of the ith component of w, denoted wi.
The author tested linear supervised learning against TD methods. To get statistically reliable results, 100 training
sets, each consisting of 10 sequences, were constructed for use by all learning procedures. The TDpλq procedure
in Equation (16.99) was used with λ  0, 0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0, 7, 0.9, 1. The true conditional probabilities for right-side
termination are 1
