2.
The root mean squared (RMS) error was used as a measure of performances. In set r1s, averaging over the training
sets, performance improved as λ was decreased from 1 to 0. This is illustrated in Figure (45).
Figure 45: Average error for random walk in case r1s
The Widrow-Hoff procedure, λ  1, only minimises error on the training set, but it does not minimises error for future
experience. In fact, Sutton proved that TDp0q converges to the optimal estimates for matching future experience,
those consistent with the maximum-likelihood estimate of the underlying Markov process.
In set r2s, WH produced the worst estimates. This is illustrated in Figure (46).
