16.3
Combining supervised learning and TD procedures
Both backpropagation (see Section (15.1.3)) and TD methods are designed to accurately assign credit.
• Backpropagation: it decides which part of a network to change in order to improve convergence to the output
by reducing the overall error of a loss function. It addresses a structural credit-assignment problem.
• TD methods: they decide how each output of a temporal sequence of outputs should be changed. They address
a temporal credit-assignment problem.
Both methods are perfectly compatible and can easily be combined. While Sutton [1988] discussed the linear case
for TD methods, they are applicable for predictions Ppxt, wq formed by nonlinear functions. The key requirement
being that the gradient ∇wPt is computable, which is the case with backpropagation. Anderson [1986] [1987]
implemented such a combination of backpropagation and TD method for solving a nonlinear broomstick-balancing
task and the Towers of Hanoi problem.
16.4
Recurrent neural networks
16.4.1
Presentation
16.4.1.1
An overview
Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) are extension of Feedforward Neural Networks (FNNs)
discussed in Section (15.1.6), where we allow connections among hidden units associated with time delay. Thus, RNNs
can retain information about the past, and, as such, discover temporal correlations between events occurring far in the
past. While the feedforward neural networks have no cycles for connections, a recurrent neural network has feedback
connection, meaning that the nodes of the network have cyclical connections between them (see Figure 48a). The existence of cycles allows RNNs to develop a self-sustained temporal activation dynamics along its recurrent connection
pathways, even in the absence of input, making them a dynamical system.
As shown in Figure 48b, RNNs can be visualised by unfolding the RNNs along the whole input sequence. That is, in
the case of time series (notion of time), the RNN are unfolded through time (see Figure ( 49)). The unfolded graph has
no cycles, which is the same as FNNs, so that the forward pass and backward pass of MLP can be applied (see Section
(15.1.3)). Recall from Section (15.1.3) that the MLP is an approximator for nonlinear functions, and since RNN can
203
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
be unfolded to a deep feedforward network, it has the advantage of the MLP, making it a better approximator.
There are two main classes of RNNs, p1q the ﬁrst one being characterised by an energy-minimising stochastic dynamics and symmetric connections (see Taylor et al.
[2007]), and p2q the second featuring a deterministic update
dynamics and directed connections. We are going to concentrate on the latter.
The biggest feature of recurrent neural network is that it has a sparsely connected hidden layer called reservoir, which
enables RNNs to have short term memory that captures information about what has been calculated so far. Usually,
this recurrent part of the structure is essential in learning complex patterns. In fact, RNNs have proved to be an
attractive form for modelling non-linearity due to their ability to approximate any dynamical system with arbitrary
precision (see Siegelmann et al. [1991]). As discussed in Section (15.1.3), Funahashi et al. [1993] showed that under
mild and general assumptions, RNNs are universal approximatiors of dynamical systems. Indeed, as an equivalent
theory of universal approximation for MLPs, it is said that a single hidden layer RNN with sufﬁcient hidden units
can approximate any sequence to arbitrary accuracy (see Hammer [2000]). Thus, the RNN is a natural generalisation
of feedforward neural networks to sequences (see). Some applications include motion capture data (see Sutskever
[2008]), text (see Sutskever et al. [2011]), music (see Boulanger-Lewandowski et al. [2012]), among others.
For simplicity of exposition, we are going to focus on a simple RNN with one self-connected hidden layer, as shown
in Figure 48a. As trivial the difference in topology between FNNs and RNNs may seems, the advantage of the RNNs
over the FNNs is profound. The latter can only map a limited number of inputs to a limited number of outputs, while
a simple RNN can map the entire time series to some outputs. This is signiﬁcant for time series prediction, as the
long history can be fed to the network, and the existence of the recursive connection allows the network to memorise
information of previous time steps, which is useful considering that ﬁnancial time series are mostly serially correlated.
