Quantitative Analytics
16.4.4
Dealing with long-range dependencies
We saw in Section (16.4.2) that recurrent neural networks (RNNs) were powerful models, since they combine two
properties:
1. Distributed hidden state allowing them to store a lot of information about the past efﬁciently.
2. Non-linear dynamics allowing them to update their hidden state in complicated ways.
In RNNs, we can specify targets in several ways:
• Specify desired ﬁnal activities of all the units.
• Specify desired activities of all units for the last few steps: It is good for learning attractors. Further, it is easy
to add in extra error derivatives as we backpropagate.
• Specify the desired activity of a subset of the units. The other units are input or hidden units.
The differences between the forward and backward passes are as follows:
• In the forward pass we use squashing functions (like the logistic) to prevent the activity vectors from exploding.
• The backward pass, is completely linear. If we double the error derivatives at the ﬁnal layer, all the error
derivatives will double.
• The forward pass determines the slope of the linear function used for backpropagating through each neuron.
However, the problem of exploding or vanishing gradients, described in Section (16.4.3), results in RNNs having
difﬁculties dealing with long-range dependencies. To go around, ﬁve effective ways of learning an RNN have been
proposed:
1. Long Short Term Memory: Build the RNN out of little modules that are designed to remember values for a long
time.
2. Hessian Free Optimisation: Deal with the vanishing gradients problem by using a fancy optimiser that can detect
directions with a tiny gradient but even smaller curvature. For example, the HF optimiser by Sutskever et al.
[2011].
3. Echo State Networks: Initialise the input, the hidden states and hidden states, the hidden states and output. The
hidden state has a huge reservoir of weakly coupled oscillators which can be selectively driven by the input.
ESNs only need to learn the connections from the hidden states to the output.
4. Good initialisation with momentum: Initialise like in Echo State Networks, but then learn all of the connections
using momentum.
5. Gradient norm clipping strategy: Dealing with the exploding gradient problem, Mikolov [2012] proposed to
rescale its norm whenever it goes over a threshold.
We will detail LSTM and ESNs in the following sections, but we ﬁrst describe their basic principles.
208
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
16.4.4.1
The LSTM
In LSTM, Hochreiter et al.
[1997] designed a memory cell using logistic and linear units
with multiplicative interactions. Information in and out of the unit is guarded by learned input and output gates. It
gets into the cell whenever its "write" gate is on. The information stays in the cell so long as its "keep" gate is on.
The information can be read from the cell by turning on its "read" gate. This over-parametrisation of the activation
function preserve memory from the distant past. As an example, reading cursive handwriting is a natural task for an
RNN. The input is a sequence of px, y, pq coordinates of the tip of the pen, where p indicates whether the pen is up or
down. The output is a sequence of characters. Graves et al. [2008] showed that RNNs with LSTM were producing
good results for reading cursive writing. They used a sequence of small images as input rather than pen coordinates.
However, as discussed in Section (16.4.3), the computational power of RNNs makes them very hard to train.
16.4.4.2
Hessian Free Optimisation
The Hessian-Free Optimiser is used in conjunction with structural damping.
The vanishing gradient problem is addressed as follows: In high dimensional spaces there is a high probability for
long term components to be orthogonal to the short term ones. It allows the Hessian to rescale these components
independently. In the case of exploding gradient structural damping is an enhancement forcing the Jacobian matrix to
have small norm.
16.4.4.3
Reservoir computing
It is easier to consider reservoir computing (Echo State Networks and Liquid State
Machines) with sparse reservoir. The key idea of ESNs is as follows: A simple way for learning a feedforward
network is to make the early layers random and ﬁxed them. Then, we just learn the last layer which is a linear model
that uses the transformed inputs to predict the target outputs. The equivalent idea for RNNs is to ﬁx the input to hidden
connections and the hidden to hidden connections at random values and only learn the hidden to output connections.
This way we do not have to learn the hidden connections. Assuming linear output units, the learning becomes very
simple. However, it is important to set the random connections very carefully so the RNN does not explode or die.
Setting the random connections in Echo State Network is as follows:
• Set the hidden to hidden weights so that the length of the activity vector stays about the same after each iteration.
It allows the input to echo around the network for a long time.
• Use sparse connectivity (set most of the weights to zero) to create lots of loosely coupled oscillators.
• Choose the scale of the input to hidden connections very carefully. They need to drive the loosely coupled
oscillators without wiping out the information from the past that they already contain.
• Since the learning is very fast, we can try many different scales for the weights and sparsenesses to improve
convergence.
Since ESNs just ﬁt a linear model, they can be trained very quickly. Further, they demonstrate that it is very important to initialise the weights sensibly. While they can do impressive modelling of one-dimensional time-series, they
experience some difﬁculties when modelling high-dimensional data such as pre-processed speech. Given a complex
task, they require more hidden units than a RNN since the latter also learn the hidden to hidden connections. In
fact, Sutskever [2012] showed that RNNs can be trained very effectively if the weights are initialised using the ESN
methods.
16.4.4.4
Gradient norm clipping strategy
We discussed in Section (16.4.3) the vanishing and the exploding
gradient problems when training recurrent neural networks. Mikolov [2012] proposed to clip the elements of the
gradient when they exceed in absolute value a ﬁxed threshold. See algorithm (17).
