We have focused on a 2-factor reservoir in our study as it provides two high dimensional signals and at the same time
keeps the optimisation time relatively low. Therefore, the number of parameters is 28. This is relatively high and
we cannot rely on an exhaustive line search, as it would take too much time to run, and also the system would be
over-optimised, leading to overﬁtting the data.
17.2.1
Random Generation: GEN
One way of solving the above problem is to consider the following functions:
- global optimisation: it generates uniformly random values inside a space globalMax times in a same loop and
update the best parameters upon the RMSE obtained on the validation sample by running training and validation.
- local optimisation: exactly the same as the global one with localMax times, except that it is now restricted to a
smaller space (20% of the global one).
- cross optimisation: it consists in optimizing the parameters one by one crossMax times and see if there is any
improvement on the validation sample. It also performs two local optimisations at the end (one at 20% followed
by one at 10%).
- While loop: it combines a more exhaustive global optimization (whileMax times) with local and cross optimisations if there is improvement in the global.
Remark 17.1 Note that at each call of any of these functions, new training and validation running are performed.
Algorithm 19 Optimisation Strategy 1: GEN
Require: get whileMax.
Require: set count Ð 0, bestRMSE Ð 8.
1: RMSE Ð global()
2: RMSE Ð local()
3: RMSE Ð cross()
4: while count   whileMax do
5:
p Ð random()
6:
node Ð Reservoir(p)
7:
prenode Ð LeakyIntegrator(node)
8:
prenode.train()
9:
RMSE Ð prenode.valid()
10:
if RMSE   bestRMSE then
11:
RMSE Ð cross()
12:
RMSE Ð local()
13:
end if
14:
count Ð count   1
15: end while
16: return RMSE
