17.3.2
Online algorithm
We are now going to describe the online algorithm for real time learning of output matrix Wout. While we have
considered the actual output of the ﬁnancial time series y, it is more relevant to take the ﬁltered signal as the target
signal. Indeed, this signal is by deﬁnition smoother and therefore more continuous in time.
Algorithm 21 RC online Training
Require: choose a gradient descent algorithm A
1: Wout Ð Algorithm ( 18)
2: set SE Ð 0
3: for t Ð 0 to Tvalid - 1 do
4:
update each reservoir factor using Equation ( 17.116).
5:
compute forecast using Equation ( 17.124).
6:
SE Ð SE   pypt   1q  ˆypt   1qq2
7:
update Wout with Equation ( 17.123) using A.
8: end for
In Algorithm ( 21) we use A as the scaled conjugate gradient implemented in GNU Scientiﬁc Library [2017]. This
method is very fast for a well-chosen initial guess and it makes sense to suppose that the output matrix should be close
to its previous state as the signal we try to calibrate is smooth.
Remark 17.4 We can also replace y in Equation ( 17.116) with its last feedback ˆy. Consequently, the reservoir has
more information about how accurate its last forecast was.
17.4
Associative Reservoir Computing
17.4.1
Paradigm
17.4.1.1
Deﬁnitions
So far, we have taught the reservoir the input to output mapping. Equation (17.128) introduce
the function φ deﬁning this mapping and carrying the forward relation between the input sample U and the output
sample Y without studying its properties. We are now going to understand the function φ in view of making better
prediction.
Deﬁnition 17.2 Ambiguous relation
Let φ : U Ñ Y . We say that φ is ambiguous if it is not injective i.e. Dpu1, u2q P U 2|u1  u2 ñ φpu1q  φpu2q
Deﬁnition 17.3 State space
A state space is the set of all possible states [the reservoir states x in our case] of a dynamical system. Each state of
the system corresponds to a unique point in the state space (see Terman et al. [2008]).
234
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
Deﬁnition 17.4 Attracting set
Let S be a dynamical system and X its associated state space. We say of a set A to be an attracting set of S if it is a
closed subset of X such that @a P A, Dxp0q  x0|xptq Ñ a as t Ñ  8.
We call its elements attractors.
Deﬁnition 17.5 Basin of attraction
Let a be an attractor of A. We deﬁne the basin of attraction of A BpAq as the set of initial conditions such that
xptq Ñ a as t Ñ  8.
In associative reservoir computing, the learning process aims at shaping the reservoir dynamics in a particular attractor.
A major problem when learning φ is that it may be an ambiguous relation. Following Reinhart [2011], we develop a
method for resolving the ambiguity of φ using a dynamical approach.
17.4.1.2
The model
In the ARC model, we overwrite the reservoir forward representation by a joint representation
involving input u and output y. To access the latter, we need a matrix Wfb  0. Furthermore, we deﬁne a matrix
mapping the reservoir states to the input layer and called it WrecpP RNUNSq. We want to learn the mapping ψ from
output to input. Figure ( 68) deﬁnes the different connections involved in this model. We have the following system:
$
