In Algorithm ( 22) we eliminate the transient effect by iterating the states using the same input. It is as if we would
have frozen the series at time t and went on feeding the reservoir, leading to convergence to an attractor. Basically, we
want to ﬁt the reservoir dynamics to this attractor in order to teach the reservoir how to reproduce its behaviour. If it is
the case, then the network should not need this trick any more and it would predict well this attractor. Ambiguity can
be resolved by applying Algorithm ( 22), causing the reservoir to settle in a particular attractor.
17.4.2
States stabilisation techniques
A major issue of our task is that only noised data are observable in the market. However, the reservoir states need to
be smooth (in the sense of its norm) in order to hope learning. Indeed, if the states are stables as time goes, then the
predicted values should be stables as well. From Table ( 2) we have seen that there is a good learning when dealing
with ﬁltered input. However we cannot use the wavelet ﬁlter in the testing sample. To offset this problem, we could
imagine teaching the reservoir how to remain consistently stable while feeding it with noisy input.
