18
SVM models
In machine learning, support vector machines (SVMs) are supervised learning models with associated learning algorithms that analyse data used for classiﬁcation (see Vapnik [1995-2000]) and regression analysis (see Drucker et al.
[1996]).
Vapnik et al.
[1963] introduced the Generalized Portrait algorithm which was further developed by Vapnik et al.
239
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
[1964]. The algorithm implemented by support vector machines is a nonlinear generalisation of the Generalized Portrait algorithm. SVM was extended by Boser et al.
[1992] who suggested a way to create nonlinear classiﬁers by
applying the kernel trick to maximum-margin hyperplanes. Cortes et al. [1995] introduced the soft margin classiﬁer.
In the same year the algorithm was extended to the case of regression by Vapnik [1995-2000].
SVM is a learning system using a high dimensional feature space where points are classiﬁed by means of assigning
them to one of the two disjoint half spaces, either in the pattern space or in a higher dimensional feature space. The
main objective is to identify maximum margin hyper plane, that is, the margin of separation between positive and
negative examples is maximised. SVM ﬁnds the maximum margin hyper plane as the ﬁnal decision boundary.
18.1
An optimisation problem
Assume that xi P Rd, i  1, ..., N forms a set of input vectors with corresponding class labels (binary variables)
y P t 1, 1u, i  1, ..., N. We want to ﬁnd the maximum margin hyperplane that divides the group of points xi
for which yi  1 from the group of points for which yi  1, which is deﬁned so that the distance between the
hyperplane and the nearest point xi from either group is maximised.
Any hyperplane can be written as the set of points x satisfying
