18.2.1.2
More complex algorithms
Recent algorithms for ﬁnding the SVM classiﬁer include sub-gradient descent
(see Shalev-Shwartz et al. [2011]) and coordinate descent (see Hsieh et al. [2008]). Both techniques have proven
to offer signiﬁcant advantages over the traditional approach when dealing with large, sparse datasets. Sub-gradient
methods are especially efﬁcient when there are many training examples, and coordinate descent when the dimension
of the feature space is high.
Sub-gradient descent algorithms for the SVM work directly with the expression fpw, bq in Equation (18.134). Since
f is a convex function, traditional gradient descent (or SGD) methods can be adapted, where instead of taking a step
in the direction of the functions gradient, a step is taken in the direction of a vector selected from the function’s subgradient.
The coordinate descent algorithms apply to the dual problem. The coefﬁcient αi, i  1, ..., N, is adjusted in the
direction of BL
