19
Probabilistic networks
19.1
The generative and discriminative approaches
In statistical classiﬁcation, including machine learning, the two main approaches are called
1. the generative approach, and
2. the discriminative approach.
These methods compute classiﬁers by using different approaches, differing in the degree of statistical modelling. In
both cases, the notion of probabilities is introduced. Jebara [2004] distinguished three major types:
1. Given an observable variable X and a target variable Y , a generative model is a statistical model of the joint
probability distribution on X and Y , denoted PpX, Y q.
2. A discriminative model is a model of the conditional probability of the target Y , given an observation x, symbolically, PpY |X  xq.
246
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
3. Classiﬁers computed without using a probability model are also referred to loosely as discriminative.
These three classes are referred to as generative learning, conditional learning, and discriminative learning. However,
Ng et al. [2002] only distinguished two classes, calling them generative classiﬁers (joint distribution) and discriminative classiﬁers (conditional distribution or no distribution), not distinguishing between the latter two classes. Analogously, a classiﬁer based on a generative model is a generative classiﬁer, while a classiﬁer based on a discriminative
model is a discriminative classiﬁer, though this term also refers to classiﬁers that are not based on a model. Standard examples of each, all of which are linear classiﬁers, are: generative classiﬁers: naive Bayes classiﬁer and linear
discriminant analysis; discriminative model: logistic regression; non-model classiﬁer: perceptron and support vector
machine.
When classifying data, we want to go from an observation x to a label y (or probability distribution on labels). We
can compute this directly, without using a probability distribution (distribution-free classiﬁer), or we can estimate
the probability of a label given an observation, PpY |X  xq (discriminative model), and base classiﬁcation on that,
or we can estimate the joint distribution PpX, Y q (generative model), from that compute the conditional probability
PpY |X  xq, and then base classiﬁcation on that. These methods are increasingly indirect, but increasingly probabilistic, allowing more domain knowledge and probability theory to be applied. In practice different approaches are
used, depending on the particular problem, and hybrids can combine strengths of multiple approaches.
More generally, a generative model is a model of the conditional probability of the observable X, given a target y,
symbolically, PpX|Y  yq, while a discriminative model is a model of the conditional probability of the target Y ,
given an observation x, symbolically, PpY |X  xq. Regardless of the precise deﬁnition, the terminology is due to the
fact that a generative model can be used to "generate" random instances (outcomes), either of an observation and target
px, yq or of an observation x given a target value y, while a discriminative model or discriminative classiﬁer (without
a model) can be used to "discriminate" the value of the target variable Y , given an observation x. The difference
between "discriminate" (distinguish) and "classify" is subtle, and these are not consistently distinguished.
19.2
Generative adversarial networks
19.2.1
Overview
As discussed in Appendix (19.1), a generative model is a statistical model of the joint probability distribution of two
observed random variables. In general, generative models focus on providing a parametric speciﬁcation of a probability distribution function (pdf). The model can then be trained by maximising the log-likelihood. However, in these
models, the pdf is generally intractable, requiring numerical approximation to compute the gradient. This is the case
of the deep Boltzmann machine (see Salakhutdinov et al.
[2009]). Recently, generative models developed representing the log-likelihood implicitely while generating samples from the desired distribution. For instance, generative
stochastic networks can be trained with exact backpropagation, but rely on Markov chains for computation. Kingma
et al. [2014] and Rezende et al. [2014] developed more general stochastic backpropagation rules, allowing one to
backpropagate through Gaussian distributions with ﬁnite variance, and to backpropagate to the covariance parameter
as well as the mean. They trained variational autoencoders (VAEs), which combines a differentiable generator network
with a second neural network. VAEs require differentiation through the hidden units.
Schmidhuber [1992b] proposed predictability minimisation having two neural networks compete. Each hidden unit
in a neural network is trained to be different from the output (a single scalar) of a second network, which predicts the
value of that hidden unit given the value of all of the other hidden units. Thus, it is a regulariser encouraging the hidden
units of a neural network to be statistically independent, while they accomplish some other task. It is an optimisation
problem with an objective function to be minimised, and learning approaches the minimum of the objective function.
Following a similar approach, Goodfellow et al. [2014] proposed GAN models which eliminate Markov chains by
observing that
lim
σÑ0 ∇xEϵNp0,σ2Iqrfpx   ϵqs  ∇xfpxq
