Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
GANs require differentiation through the visible units, and thus cannot model discrete data. In GANs, one network
produces a rich, high dimensional vector that is used as the input to another network, and attempts to choose an input
that the other network does not know how to process. Here, the competition between the networks is the only training
criterion. It is based on a minimax game with a value function that one agent seeks to maximise and the other seeks to
minimise. The game terminates at a saddle point corresponding to a minimum for one player and a maximum for the
other player.
19.2.2
Description
A generative adversarial network (GAN) model (see Goodfellow et al.
[2014]) is a novel approach for training
generative models. They estimate the latter via an adversarial process where two models are simultaneously trained:
1. Generator: A a generative net G captures the data distribution by learning to confuse D by generating high
quality data. It is acting as a team of counterfeiters.
2. Discriminator: A discriminative net D that estimates the probability that a sample came from the training data
(real) rather than G. It learns to separate the data created by G from the observed data. It acts as the police
trying to catch the counterfeiters.
The training process for G is to maximise the probability that D makes a mistake (incapacity of recognising true from
generated data), which corresponds to a minmax two-player game. In order for G to confuse D as much as possible, it
must reduce the adversarial loss in the sense that D will not correctly discriminate the prediction. The target loss for
D is equal to the adversarial loss on D (D is able to classify correctly inputs from G as fake and the dataset as true).
Through competition, both networks improve their performance until the genuine data is indistinguishable from the
generated one. If the pair pG, Dq is made of multilayer perceptrons, the system can be trained with backpropagation.
We present the adversarial nets, where both G and D are multilayer perceptrons, and G generates samples by passing
random noise as input. We let z be the input noise variables with prior pZpzq, and want to learn (implicitly) the
distribution pG over data x. The generator is the neural network Gpz, θGq with the parameter θG. It maps data to the
data-input space G : z Ñ x. The Discriminator is the neural network Dpx, θDq that scores the likelihood that x was
sampled from the known dataset, pdatapxq  D : x Ñ r0, 1s. D is trained to maximise correct labelling, while G is
trained to minimise log p1  DpGpzqqq. Note, there is no explicit representation for pGpxq since it is expressed via
Gp, θGq. As such Markov chains are not needed.
Thus, the pair pG, Dq plays a two-player minmax game with value function V pG, Dq given by
