Since optimising D in an inner loop is expensive, the authors proposed to alternate between k steps of optimising D
and one step of optimising G, to maintain D near its optimal solution as long as G slowly evolves. In the early stage of
the process, G is poor and D has a high accuracy at rejecting the samples created from G, leading log p1  DpGpzqqq
to saturate. One solution is to train G to maximise log DpGpzqq, which provides stronger gradient in the early stage.
