19.2.4
Improvements
In general, GANs are good at learning an unconditional model such as image and text creation or discovery. When
it comes to learning ﬁnancial time series, one require a sampling process taking into account the previous state space
to preserve the statistical properties of the series. As a solution, Goodfellow et al.
[2014]) proposed to consider
Conditional GANs which can handle dependent data generation. In that setting, ppx|cq can be obtained by adding c
as input to the pair pG, Dq. Further, they showed that one could perform learned approximate inference by training
an auxiliary network to predict z given x. While similar to the inference net trained by the wake-sleep algorithm (see
Hinton et al. [1995]), here the inference net may be trained for a ﬁxed generator net after the generator net has ﬁnished
training.
Over time new architectures, loss functions, training, among others, developed to improve the original model. Some
of these are as follows:
• Ensemble strategies: Train multiple GANs with different initial conditions, slices of data and tasks to attain.
The output from the generators are aggregated with some operators (summing, weighted average) from multiple
checkpoints or at the end of the training. Examples of these steps are Stacked GANs (see Huang et al. [2017]),
Ensembles of GANs (see Wang et al. [2016b]), AdaGANS (see Tolstikhin et al. [2017]), among others.
• Loss function reshaping: Reshape the original loss function to circumvent problems related to training instability.
250
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
• Adjusting architecture and training process: As an example, the Deep Convolutional GAN (see Radford et al.
[2015]) has a set of constraints on the architectural topology of Convolutional GANs to make the training more
stable. Another example is the Evolutionary GANs (see Wang et al. [2018]) that adds to the training loop of a
GAN different metrics to jointly optimise the generator. It also employs a population of generators, created by
introducing novel mutation operators.
• Bidirectional GAN (BiGAN): It aims at introducing a generator model to act as the discriminator, whereby
the discriminator naturally considers the entire translation space so that the inadequate training problem can be
alleviated (see Zhang et al. [2018b]). To satisfy this property, generator and discriminator are both designed
to model the joint probability of sentence pairs, with the difference that, the generator decomposes the joint
probability with a source language model and a source-to-target translation model, while the discriminator is
formulated as a target language model and a target-to-source translation model. To further leverage the symmetry of them, an auxiliary GAN is introduced and adopts generator and discriminator models of original one as
its own discriminator and generator respectively. Two GANs are alternately trained to update the parameters.
19.2.5
Conditional generative adversarial networks
Conditional GANs (cGANs) are an extension of GANs (see Mirza et al. [2014]), where the pair pG, Dq is not only
based in noise or generated inputs, but includes an additional information set V . It can represent a class label, a certain
categorical feature, or an expected market condition. That is, cGANs try to learn an implicit conditional generative
model. It can be constructed by feeding the data we wish to condition on, to both the generator and the discriminator.
We let v be the conditional variable and modify the GAN model as follows: G : z  v Ñ x and D : x  v Ñ r0, 1s.
Now, G is trained to minimise log
